<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="description" content="Space Explorer Issue 05: an illustrated Grade 5 reader about constellations, with an Orion line toggle, language support and a four-page PDF."><meta name="theme-color" content="#0c2234"><title>Constellations · Space Explorer Issue 05</title><link rel="icon" href="assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css"><script src="translations.js" defer></script><script src="app.js" defer></script><link rel="stylesheet" href="../reader-navigation.css"></head><body><aside class="space-hub-bar" aria-label="Space Explorer topics"><a class="space-hub-home" href="../">← All space topics</a><div class="space-hub-links"><a href="../mars/">Mars</a><a href="../moon/">Earth’s Moon</a><a href="../iss/">International Space Station</a><a href="../sun/">The Sun</a><a href="../constellations/" aria-current="page">Constellations</a></div></aside><a class="skip" href="#reader">Skip to the reader</a><header class="masthead"><a class="brand" href="#top">SPACE EXPLORER<span>THE GRADE 5 SCIENCE READER</span></a><span class="issue">ISSUE <b>05</b></span><a class="pdf-link" href="constellations-reader.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open illustrated PDF ↗</a></header><nav class="chapters" aria-label="Reader sections"><a href="#p1"><span>01</span> Star pictures</a><a href="#p2"><span>02</span> Perspective</a><a href="#p3"><span>03</span> A changing sky</a><a href="#p4"><span>04</span> Finding north</a><a href="#mission">Mission words</a></nav><main id="reader"><div class="cover" id="top"><p class="eyebrow">LOOK UP. LOOK CLOSER.</p><h1>CONSTELLATIONS</h1><p class="deck">Discovering the Patterns and Secrets<br class="wide-only"> of the Night Sky</p><div class="cover-meta"><span>GRADE 5</span><span>4 SECTIONS</span><span>WIDA 2–4</span></div></div><section class="reader-tools" aria-label="Language and read-aloud support"><div class="tool-label">YOUR READING TOOLS</div><div class="tool-row"><label>Home language<select id="language"><option value="en">English</option><option value="zh-CN">简体中文 · Chinese</option><option value="ko-KR">한국어 · Korean</option><option value="ja-JP">日本語 · Japanese</option><option value="de-DE">Deutsch · German</option></select></label><label>Read section<select id="section-select"><option value="0">01 · Pictures in the Stars</option><option value="1">02 · The Secret Behind the Pattern</option><option value="2">03 · A Sky That Changes</option><option value="3">04 · Stars That Guide Us</option></select></label><label>Reading voice<select id="voice"><option value="">Device default</option></select></label><div class="speech-buttons"><button id="read" type="button">Read section</button><button id="pause" type="button" disabled>Pause</button><button id="stop" type="button" disabled>Stop</button></div></div><p id="speech-status" role="status">English: hear the current section. Home language: hear a short section summary.</p><p class="support-note">Language supports help you read the English. Translations are drafts for teacher review. Voices depend on your browser and device.</p><aside id="summary" hidden><strong>Home-language summary · section <span id="summary-number">1</span></strong><p id="summary-text"></p></aside></section><noscript><p class="noscript">The complete English reader and PDF work without JavaScript. Enable JavaScript for the Orion toggle, translations and read-aloud.</p></noscript><section id="p1" class="chapter" aria-labelledby="title-1"><div class="chapter-heading"><span class="chapter-number">01</span><div><p class="eyebrow">DISCOVER</p><h2 id="title-1">Pictures in the Stars</h2><p class="question-heading">Can three stars tell a story?</p></div></div><div class="chapter-grid"><div class="visual"><figure class="orion-figure"><div class="photo-stage"><img id="orion-photo" src="assets/images/orion.jpg" width="1598" height="2000" alt="Ground-based photograph of Orion: three bright Belt stars form a short sloping row between reddish Betelgeuse, bluish Bellatrix, Saiph and bright Rigel. No lines have been added to the photograph."/><img id="orion-lines" src="assets/orion-overlay.svg" width="1598" height="2000" alt="" hidden></div><div class="figure-tools"><button id="toggle-lines" type="button" aria-pressed="false" aria-controls="orion-lines" hidden>Show constellation lines</button><p id="line-description" aria-live="polite">Original photograph. Look for the three bright Belt stars near the middle.</p></div><figcaption>REAL SKY PHOTOGRAPH · Credit: Akira Fujii, via <a href="https://esahubble.org/images/opo0205b/">ESA/Hubble</a> · <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>. Optional educational lines connect seven identified Orion stars; they are imagined connections, not physical links.</figcaption></figure></div><div class="reading"><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="0-0">Imagine standing outside on a clear night.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="0-0" aria-controls="translation-0-0" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-0-0" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="0-1">You look up and notice three bright stars in a row. Nearby, other stars seem to form the shape of a person.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="0-1" aria-controls="translation-0-1" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-0-1" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="0-2">But are you really looking at a giant person in the sky?</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="0-2" aria-controls="translation-0-2" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-0-2" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="0-3">Of course not! You&#x27;re looking at a pattern of stars.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="0-3" aria-controls="translation-0-3" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-0-3" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="0-4">For thousands of years, people around the world have looked at stars and imagined animals, people, and objects. These familiar patterns are often called <strong>constellations</strong>.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="0-4" aria-controls="translation-0-4" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-0-4" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="0-5">One famous constellation is <strong>Orion</strong>. In ancient Greek stories, Orion was a hunter. His three bright belt stars make him easier to recognize.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="0-5" aria-controls="translation-0-5" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-0-5" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="0-6">Different cultures have imagined different pictures among the stars. The Big Dipper, for example, is a familiar star pattern known as the Northern Dipper in traditional Chinese astronomy.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="0-6" aria-controls="translation-0-6" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-0-6" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="0-7">Today, astronomers use 88 officially named constellations to divide the sky into regions, like sections of a giant map.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="0-7" aria-controls="translation-0-7" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-0-7" hidden></p></div><aside class="callout"><strong>LOOK CLOSELY</strong><p>Find Orion&#x27;s three belt stars in the photograph. Can you trace another pattern around them?</p></aside><aside class="callout"><strong>BIG QUESTION</strong><p>Why might people from different cultures see different pictures in the same stars?</p></aside></div></div><div class="section-end"><span>SPACE EXPLORER · CONSTELLATIONS</span><a href="#p2">Next section →</a><b>01 / 04</b></div></section><section id="p2" class="chapter" aria-labelledby="title-2"><div class="chapter-heading"><span class="chapter-number">02</span><div><p class="eyebrow">EXPLORE</p><h2 id="title-2">The Secret Behind the Pattern</h2><p class="question-heading">Are the stars really close together?</p></div></div><div class="chapter-grid"><div class="visual"><figure><img src="assets/perspective.svg" width="800" height="480" alt="Conceptual diagram: stars A, B and C appear in a row from Earth. From above, their different depths become clear. Illustrative stars, not Orion coordinates; not to scale."><figcaption>SCIENTIFIC DIAGRAM · A perspective model, not an actual 3D map of Orion. <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/asset/hubble/the-true-shape-of-orion/">Explore NASA’s 3D explanation</a>.</figcaption></figure><aside class="distance-note"><strong>Orion’s Belt · approximate distance estimates</strong><dl><div><dt>Alnitak</dt><dd>~800 light-years</dd></div><div><dt>Alnilam</dt><dd>~1,300 light-years</dd></div><div><dt>Mintaka</dt><dd>~900 light-years</dd></div></dl><p>Published estimates differ. These are rounded classroom values, not exact measurements or a scale drawing.</p></aside></div><div class="reading"><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="1-0">Look at Orion&#x27;s Belt. Three stars seem to form one straight line.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="1-0" aria-controls="translation-1-0" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-1-0" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="1-1">But here&#x27;s a surprise: those stars are not sitting next to one another in space!</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="1-1" aria-controls="translation-1-1" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-1-1" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="1-2">From Earth, they appear close because of our <strong>perspective</strong>—the way objects look from where we are standing.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="1-2" aria-controls="translation-1-2" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-1-2" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="1-3">Imagine three friends standing far apart on a playground. From one position, they might look as if they&#x27;re standing in a straight line. Walk to the side, and the pattern changes.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="1-3" aria-controls="translation-1-3" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-1-3" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="1-4">The same idea helps us understand constellations.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="1-4" aria-controls="translation-1-4" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-1-4" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="1-5">Stars are so far away that scientists measure their distances in <strong>light-years</strong>. A light-year is the distance light travels in one year. It measures distance, not time.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="1-5" aria-controls="translation-1-5" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-1-5" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="1-6">The three bright stars in Orion&#x27;s Belt are approximately 800, 1,300, and 900 light-years from Earth. That means they are separated by enormous distances, even though they seem to form a neat row in the sky.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="1-6" aria-controls="translation-1-6" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-1-6" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="1-7">If we could travel far away and look at Orion from another direction, the pattern would look different.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="1-7" aria-controls="translation-1-7" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-1-7" hidden></p></div><aside class="callout"><strong>WOW FACT</strong><p>Light from Orion&#x27;s stars takes hundreds of years to reach Earth. When we look at those stars tonight, we are seeing light that started traveling long before we were born!</p></aside><aside class="callout"><strong>THINK LIKE A SCIENTIST</strong><p>How can three stars seem close together when they are actually so far apart?</p></aside></div></div><div class="section-end"><span>SPACE EXPLORER · CONSTELLATIONS</span><a href="#p3">Next section →</a><b>02 / 04</b></div></section><section id="p3" class="chapter" aria-labelledby="title-3"><div class="chapter-heading"><span class="chapter-number">03</span><div><p class="eyebrow">INVESTIGATE</p><h2 id="title-3">A Sky That Changes</h2><p class="question-heading">Why do the stars seem to move?</p></div></div><div class="chapter-grid"><div class="visual"><figure class="trail"><img src="assets/images/star-trails.jpg" width="1200" height="1800" loading="lazy" alt="Long-exposure photograph above ESO’s Paranal Observatory: many star trails arc across the sky over desert hills, alongside straight observatory laser beams."><figcaption>REAL LONG-EXPOSURE PHOTOGRAPH · Star trails record apparent motion as Earth rotates. The straight yellow beams are observatory lasers. Credit: <a href="https://www.facebook.com/PetrHoralekPhotography/">P. Horálek</a>/ESO · <a href="https://www.eso.org/public/images/2019-07-05-Prnl-DistStartrails-fromArmazones-CC/">Original record</a> · CC BY 4.0. Resized.</figcaption></figure><figure class="motion"><img src="assets/earth-motion.svg" width="900" height="440" loading="lazy" alt="Two schematics: Earth spins on its axis daily; Earth orbits the Sun yearly. At opposite orbital positions the night side faces different directions. Not to scale, tilt omitted."><figcaption>SCIENTIFIC SCHEMATICS · Rotation and orbit are different motions. Distant stars are outside this view.</figcaption></figure></div><div class="reading"><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="2-0">Look at the stars early in the evening. Then look again a few hours later.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="2-0" aria-controls="translation-2-0" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-2-0" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="2-1">They appear to have moved across the sky!</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="2-1" aria-controls="translation-2-1" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-2-1" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="2-2">Most of this apparent movement happens because <strong>Earth rotates</strong>, or spins.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="2-2" aria-controls="translation-2-2" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-2-2" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="2-3">As Earth turns, we face different directions in space. The stars seem to move, much as buildings seem to move past you when you&#x27;re riding in a car.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="2-3" aria-controls="translation-2-3" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-2-3" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="2-4">But something else happens throughout the year.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="2-4" aria-controls="translation-2-4" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-2-4" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="2-5">Earth also <strong>orbits the Sun</strong>. As Earth moves around the Sun, we face different parts of space at night during different seasons.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="2-5" aria-controls="translation-2-5" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-2-5" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="2-6">That&#x27;s why some constellations are easier to see during certain months.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="2-6" aria-controls="translation-2-6" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-2-6" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="2-7">For example, Orion is a familiar sight on many winter evenings in the Northern Hemisphere. During other seasons, it is harder to see because its position in the sky is closer to the Sun during the day.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="2-7" aria-controls="translation-2-7" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-2-7" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="2-8">Your location matters, too. People living in different parts of Earth may see different constellations.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="2-8" aria-controls="translation-2-8" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-2-8" hidden></p></div><aside class="callout"><strong>REMEMBER</strong><p>Earth rotates → stars appear to move across the sky during the night. Earth orbits the Sun → different constellations become visible at night during different seasons.</p></aside><aside class="callout"><strong>BIG QUESTION</strong><p>Why might you see Orion on a winter evening but not on a summer evening?</p></aside></div></div><div class="section-end"><span>SPACE EXPLORER · CONSTELLATIONS</span><a href="#p4">Next section →</a><b>03 / 04</b></div></section><section id="p4" class="chapter" aria-labelledby="title-4"><div class="chapter-heading"><span class="chapter-number">04</span><div><p class="eyebrow">CONNECT</p><h2 id="title-4">Stars That Guide Us</h2><p class="question-heading">Can the stars help you find your way?</p></div></div><div class="chapter-grid"><div class="visual"><figure><img src="assets/polaris.svg" width="800" height="490" loading="lazy" alt="Big Dipper sky guide: follow Merak through Dubhe, then extend onward to Polaris in Ursa Minor. The Big Dipper is an asterism within Ursa Major. Orientation changes with time."><figcaption>SCIENTIFIC STAR CHART · Redrawn from <a href="https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/what-is-the-north-star-and-how-do-you-find-it/">NASA/JPL-Caltech’s verified guide</a>. Follow Merak → Dubhe → Polaris. Most useful in Northern Hemisphere skies. <a href="assets/images/polaris-nasa.jpg">View NASA’s original diagram</a>.</figcaption></figure></div><div class="reading"><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="3-0">Imagine traveling across the ocean hundreds of years ago.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="3-0" aria-controls="translation-3-0" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-3-0" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="3-1">There are no satellites or GPS systems to guide you. How could you find your direction?</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="3-1" aria-controls="translation-3-1" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-3-1" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="3-2">For generations, travelers have used the Sun and stars to help them navigate. <strong>Navigation</strong> means finding where you are and which direction to travel.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="3-2" aria-controls="translation-3-2" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-3-2" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="3-3">One useful star is <strong>Polaris</strong>, the North Star.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="3-3" aria-controls="translation-3-3" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-3-3" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="3-4">From much of the Northern Hemisphere, Polaris appears in almost the same place in the night sky. It is close to the direction Earth&#x27;s North Pole points in space.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="3-4" aria-controls="translation-3-4" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-3-4" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="3-5">You can find Polaris using two bright stars at the edge of the Big Dipper&#x27;s bowl. Draw an imaginary line through those stars, and it points toward Polaris.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="3-5" aria-controls="translation-3-5" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-3-5" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="3-6">The Big Dipper is an <em>asterism</em>, a recognizable star pattern that belongs to the larger constellation Ursa Major.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="3-6" aria-controls="translation-3-6" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-3-6" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="3-7">For thousands of years, people have also used the sky to tell stories, track seasons, and share knowledge.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="3-7" aria-controls="translation-3-7" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-3-7" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="3-8">Today, scientists use constellation regions like sections of a map to locate stars and other objects in space.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="3-8" aria-controls="translation-3-8" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-3-8" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="3-9">The next time you look up, remember that the night sky is more than a collection of lights.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="3-9" aria-controls="translation-3-9" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-3-9" hidden></p></div><div class="paragraph"><p class="readpara" data-paragraph="3-10">It is a place filled with patterns, distant stars, human stories, and scientific mysteries.</p><button type="button" class="translate" data-translate="3-10" aria-controls="translation-3-10" aria-expanded="false" hidden>Translate this paragraph</button><p class="translation" id="translation-3-10" hidden></p></div><aside class="callout"><strong>MISSION QUESTION</strong><p>How can the same stars help people tell stories, find directions, and explore space?</p></aside></div></div><div class="section-end"><span>SPACE EXPLORER · CONSTELLATIONS</span><a href="#mission">Explore mission words →</a><b>04 / 04</b></div></section><section id="mission" class="mission" aria-labelledby="mission-title"><p class="eyebrow">BUILD YOUR SCIENCE LANGUAGE</p><h2 id="mission-title">Five mission words</h2><div class="word-grid"><article class="word"><a class="word-visual" href="#p1" aria-label="Explore the constellation visual"><img src="assets/images/orion.jpg" alt="Orion photograph" class="word-photo"></a><h3>Constellation</h3><p>An officially named area of the sky, often associated with a star pattern</p><p class="frame"><strong>Say it:</strong> A constellation is ______.</p></article><article class="word"><a class="word-visual" href="#p1" aria-label="Explore the pattern visual"><svg viewBox="0 0 250 90" aria-label="Three stars in a row" role="img"><path d="M45 45H205" stroke="#7cc4cd" stroke-width="3"/><g fill="#ffdc95"><circle cx="45" cy="45" r="9"/><circle cx="125" cy="45" r="9"/><circle cx="205" cy="45" r="9"/></g></svg></a><h3>Pattern</h3><p>An arrangement of things we can recognize</p><p class="frame"><strong>Say it:</strong> I recognize the pattern of ______ because ______.</p></article><article class="word"><a class="word-visual" href="#p2" aria-label="Explore the perspective visual"><img src="assets/perspective.svg" alt="Two views of illustrative stars"></a><h3>Perspective</h3><p>The way something looks from a particular position</p><p class="frame"><strong>Say it:</strong> From a different perspective, ______.</p></article><article class="word"><a class="word-visual" href="#p2" aria-label="Explore the light-year visual"><svg viewBox="0 0 250 90" role="img" aria-label="Light travels a distance in one year"><path d="M30 45H220" stroke="#ffdc95" stroke-width="3"/><circle cx="30" cy="45" r="8" fill="#fff"/><text x="52" y="31" fill="#fff" font-size="14">light travels for 1 year</text><text x="85" y="73" fill="#fff" font-size="14">= a distance</text></svg></a><h3>Light-year</h3><p>The distance light travels in one year</p><p class="frame"><strong>Say it:</strong> A light-year measures ______.</p></article><article class="word"><a class="word-visual" href="#p4" aria-label="Explore the navigation visual"><img src="assets/polaris.svg" alt="Pointer stars lead to Polaris"></a><h3>Navigation</h3><p>Finding your position and the direction to travel</p><p class="frame"><strong>Say it:</strong> People use stars for navigation by ______.</p></article></div><div class="reflect"><div><p class="eyebrow">TALK. REASON. EXPLAIN.</p><h2>Think like a scientist</h2><ol><li>How can you recognize Orion in the night sky?</li><li>Why do stars in a constellation sometimes look close together even when they are far apart?</li><li>What is the difference between a constellation and an asterism?</li><li>Why do we see different constellations at different times of the year?</li><li>How can the stars help people find directions?</li></ol></div><aside><h3>Partner sentence frames</h3><p>I recognize ______ because ______.</p><p>The stars look ______, but actually ______.</p><p>From a different perspective, ______.</p><p>We see different constellations because ______.</p><p>People use stars for navigation by ______.</p><a class="pdf-link" href="constellations-reader.pdf">Take the four-page PDF ↗</a></aside></div></section><footer class="site-footer"><div><strong>SPACE EXPLORER · ISSUE 05</strong><p>Constellations · Grade 5 · Educational review edition</p></div><div><a href="credits.html">Sources, photo credits & science notes</a><a href="TEST-REPORT.md">Testing & review notes</a><a href="#top">Back to top ↑</a></div></footer></main></body></html>